.TH POLARIS-SENSORD 8 "2026" "polaris" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
polaris-sensord \- publish the sensor readings that only root can read
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B polaris-sensord
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The kernel publishes some readings to root only. The CPU package power counter in
.IR /sys/class/powercap
is the one Polaris wanted first: rather than opening that file to every user,
.B polaris-sensord
samples it and publishes the result as named values in
.IR /run/polaris/sensors ,
where the Polaris dock item reads it.
.PP
The file is written atomically, replaced on every sample, and left in place if the
service stops: the dock item only believes a reading while it is fresh, so a
stopped service degrades to "no reading" rather than to a stale one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-powercap\-root " path"
Read the energy counters from somewhere other than
.IR /sys/class/powercap .
Intended for testing the service without privileges.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " path"
Write the readings to somewhere other than
.IR /run/polaris/sensors .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/polaris/sensors
The published readings, one
.I key=value
pair per line, with
.I time
in seconds since the epoch. Unknown keys are ignored by readers, so this service
can publish more readings without older readers having to know about them.
.SH SECURITY
The service runs as root and is deliberately narrow. It reads three sysfs files
and writes one file of its own, and it takes no input from any client: there is no
socket, no bus name and no configuration to parse, so the set of readings it can
hand out is fixed at build time. It publishes named values and never accepts a
path or a command.
.PP
Its unit file sets
.IR NoNewPrivileges ,
.IR ProtectSystem=strict
with
.IR ReadWritePaths=/run/polaris ,
.IR ProtectHome
and no network, so it cannot write outside its own directory even if it were
misconfigured.
The published readings are not secret — they are power figures — which is why the
file is world-readable rather than restricted.
